1
00:00:00,112 --> 00:00:01,972
What's also worth knowing about resume and

2
00:00:02,100 --> 00:00:03,960
that picker you get when you run that,

3
00:00:04,586 --> 00:00:07,007
or of course, if you run py

4
00:00:07,633 --> 00:00:08,996
-r, which gives you that same picker,

5
00:00:09,637 --> 00:00:12,027
is that there also are some shortcuts you

6
00:00:12,059 --> 00:00:14,159
can use here, which are easy to overlook,

7
00:00:14,384 --> 00:00:15,715
but they are interesting.

8
00:00:16,372 --> 00:00:19,034
For example, you can use the Tab key to

9
00:00:19,034 --> 00:00:20,574
change the scope to

10
00:00:21,119 --> 00:00:23,524
see more sessions than just the current

11
00:00:23,524 --> 00:00:26,539
folder. The current folder is the default,

12
00:00:26,956 --> 00:00:27,886
As you also see here.

13
00:00:28,575 --> 00:00:31,108
But if I hit Tab, you see that switches to

14
00:00:31,284 --> 00:00:31,332
All

15
00:00:31,925 --> 00:00:34,234
and I can resume any session from any

16
00:00:34,250 --> 00:00:37,344
project. That is typically not that useful

17
00:00:37,408 --> 00:00:39,940
if you are working in a specific project and

18
00:00:39,940 --> 00:00:42,040
you want to continue your work there. But it

19
00:00:42,040 --> 00:00:45,054
can be helpful if you're running Py globally

20
00:00:45,086 --> 00:00:47,362
on your system from different folders to,

21
00:00:47,491 --> 00:00:49,879
for example, do data analytics work or

22
00:00:49,879 --> 00:00:52,268
anything like that. And you remember that

23
00:00:52,284 --> 00:00:55,089
you used it once in some folder to do

24
00:00:55,089 --> 00:00:56,996
something, but you're not quite sure which

25
00:00:57,173 --> 00:00:59,290
folder that was. Well, Well, in such cases,

26
00:00:59,354 --> 00:01:01,345
you can switch to the global scope and

27
00:01:01,361 --> 00:01:03,497
browse through all your sessions.

28
00:01:04,573 --> 00:01:06,820
And browsing through sessions can also be

29
00:01:06,820 --> 00:01:09,534
simplified because you can actually search

30
00:01:09,534 --> 00:01:09,791
here.

31
00:01:10,738 --> 00:01:14,223
If I search for create, for example, you see

32
00:01:14,351 --> 00:01:16,503
that session here gets prioritized because

33
00:01:16,599 --> 00:01:18,333
it has the word create in there.

34
00:01:18,943 --> 00:01:20,709
The same would be true if I type vanilla

35
00:01:20,709 --> 00:01:22,813
because that is also in there in that

36
00:01:22,813 --> 00:01:23,584
session title.

37
00:01:24,642 --> 00:01:28,474
If I type first or demo, then of course this

38
00:01:28,474 --> 00:01:29,964
one stays first because, well,

39
00:01:30,558 --> 00:01:32,946
those words are in there. So if you know you

40
00:01:32,962 --> 00:01:34,613
assigned a name or you

41
00:01:35,286 --> 00:01:37,787
remember that that automatically assigned

42
00:01:37,787 --> 00:01:39,807
title here, which is your initial prompt by

43
00:01:39,807 --> 00:01:42,244
the way, contains certain words, you can

44
00:01:42,276 --> 00:01:45,177
just quickly search for them here to quickly

45
00:01:45,177 --> 00:01:47,453
select that session you want to resume. You

46
00:01:47,469 --> 00:01:50,980
can use Ctrl+N to only show named sessions,

47
00:01:51,236 --> 00:01:52,711
which of course again can be useful if you

48
00:01:52,711 --> 00:01:54,459
know that a certain session has a name,

49
00:01:55,164 --> 00:01:57,559
and you can use Ctrl+ to delete a session.

50
00:01:58,235 --> 00:02:00,473
You will have to hit Enter to confirm that

51
00:02:00,473 --> 00:02:02,808
or Escape to cancel, but that allows you to

52
00:02:02,808 --> 00:02:05,013
get rid of a session and the respective

53
00:02:05,013 --> 00:02:07,251
session file on your disk if you know that

54
00:02:07,251 --> 00:02:08,377
you don't need it anymore.

55
00:02:09,182 --> 00:02:12,078
Now, speaking of sessions on disk,

56
00:02:12,910 --> 00:02:16,737
Pi stores your sessions on disk. If you go

57
00:02:16,737 --> 00:02:19,417
to your global Pi agent folder,

58
00:02:21,649 --> 00:02:23,334
you'll have a sessions folder in there.

59
00:02:23,973 --> 00:02:26,619
And I have a lot of subfolders there because

60
00:02:26,619 --> 00:02:28,865
I've been using Py in a lot of different

61
00:02:28,865 --> 00:02:29,490
projects.

62
00:02:30,308 --> 00:02:30,421
Now

63
00:02:30,950 --> 00:02:33,147
here, for example, is that subfolder for

64
00:02:33,260 --> 00:02:35,858
that demo I worked on. The naming of that

65
00:02:35,858 --> 00:02:38,216
subfolder is simply a normalized version of

66
00:02:38,248 --> 00:02:39,900
the path you ran Py in.

67
00:02:40,734 --> 00:02:43,060
And in that folder, you'll find one file per

68
00:02:43,060 --> 00:02:45,129
session. So here I only have one session

69
00:02:45,145 --> 00:02:47,246
because I deleted one, but if I quickly

70
00:02:47,246 --> 00:02:49,235
start a new session and send a message to

71
00:02:49,235 --> 00:02:51,513
populate it with a message and a response,

72
00:02:52,827 --> 00:02:54,237
You'll see I got 2 files in there,

73
00:02:54,829 --> 00:02:56,095
one per session.

74
00:02:56,768 --> 00:02:59,043
Now, if you open such a session file with

75
00:02:59,235 --> 00:03:01,494
any code or text editor of your choice,

76
00:03:02,039 --> 00:03:04,906
you'll see that the session format Pi uses

77
00:03:05,339 --> 00:03:08,270
is this JSONL format where you have a JSON

78
00:03:08,367 --> 00:03:11,747
object per line. And every line represents

79
00:03:11,747 --> 00:03:15,992
one turn or one action in that session. That

80
00:03:15,992 --> 00:03:18,812
includes stuff like you changing a model or

81
00:03:18,860 --> 00:03:21,023
the thinking level, which I did here. But of

82
00:03:21,103 --> 00:03:23,474
course, it also includes the messages. That

83
00:03:23,522 --> 00:03:25,990
were sent by you or the assistant. So here,

84
00:03:26,006 --> 00:03:27,866
this message, for example, that is my

85
00:03:27,914 --> 00:03:29,677
message to create an HTML file.

86
00:03:30,398 --> 00:03:31,424
And you see every

87
00:03:32,001 --> 00:03:33,620
entry here has an ID,

88
00:03:34,165 --> 00:03:36,345
has a parent ID, something which will also

89
00:03:36,345 --> 00:03:38,877
become important later, a timestamp, and

90
00:03:38,909 --> 00:03:41,666
then some details like if it was sent by the

91
00:03:41,778 --> 00:03:44,439
user, by you, by the AI assistant, or if it

92
00:03:44,439 --> 00:03:46,555
was a tool result or anything like that.

93
00:03:47,228 --> 00:03:47,356
Now,

94
00:03:47,965 --> 00:03:50,017
I'm not showing you this because you should

95
00:03:50,193 --> 00:03:50,802
edit this file.

96
00:03:51,380 --> 00:03:53,770
Typically not do that, but because it's

97
00:03:53,962 --> 00:03:56,561
interesting to see where those sessions are

98
00:03:56,690 --> 00:03:58,711
actually stored. Also, in case you want to

99
00:03:58,711 --> 00:04:01,390
mass clean up your sessions, for example,

100
00:04:01,454 --> 00:04:03,524
it's way quicker to simply delete the

101
00:04:03,524 --> 00:04:05,850
folders here than by opening them all up and

102
00:04:05,850 --> 00:04:08,064
deleting messages individually from inside

103
00:04:08,096 --> 00:04:10,438
Pi. And because later when we dive into the

104
00:04:10,438 --> 00:04:11,753
more advanced Pi features,

105
00:04:12,331 --> 00:04:15,652
we'll get back to that way of Pi saving

106
00:04:15,716 --> 00:04:17,689
sessions and how that can benefit us.
